# Purpose

This document defines the engineering review standards that every implementation must satisfy before it is considered complete.

Its purpose is to ensure that all generated code, documentation, and architectural decisions consistently meet the quality expectations established by the Engineering Constitution.

---

# Scope

This standard defines:

* Engineering review process
* Review categories
* Quality expectations
* Production readiness criteria
* Definition of review completion

It intentionally excludes implementation guidance, which belongs to the relevant Engineering Standards.

---

# Constitutional Interpretation

This document extends the hubIO Engineering Constitution.

It translates constitutional engineering principles into a practical review process that verifies implementation quality before completion.

---

# Required Reading

1. Engineering Constitution

Recommended:

* Project Standards
* Product Standards
* Architecture Standards

---

# Engineering Responsibilities

This document owns:

* Engineering review process
* Quality evaluation
* Production readiness verification
* Review consistency

It does not define implementation approaches or engineering architecture.

---

# Review Philosophy

Every implementation should be reviewed against the Engineering Constitution before it is considered complete.

The objective of review is not to find faults, but to improve quality, maintain consistency, and reduce long-term maintenance costs.

Review should verify both technical correctness and product alignment.

Every review should answer a single question:

> Is this implementation ready to become part of the production system?

If the answer is uncertain, further refinement is required.


---

# Review Process

Every implementation should follow a consistent review process before completion.

The review process should verify that the implementation satisfies business requirements, engineering standards, architectural constraints, and production quality expectations.

A typical review consists of the following stages:

1. Business Validation
2. Architecture Review
3. Engineering Review
4. User Experience Review
5. Performance Review
6. Security Review
7. Production Readiness Verification

Each stage should be completed before progressing to the next.

Review should improve implementation quality rather than simply detect mistakes.

---

# Business Review

Verify that the implementation solves the intended business problem.

Review should confirm:

* Business requirements are fully satisfied.
* Product scope is respected.
* Product principles remain consistent.
* User workflows remain intuitive.
* Future product growth is supported.
* Business terminology remains consistent.

An implementation that works technically but fails the business objective is considered incomplete.

---

# Architecture Review

Verify that the implementation follows the architectural standards of the project.

Review should confirm:

* Architectural boundaries are respected.
* Dependencies remain appropriate.
* Layer responsibilities remain clear.
* Multi-tenant assumptions remain valid.
* Business logic is correctly separated.
* Shared components are reused whenever appropriate.

Architectural consistency is more valuable than short-term implementation convenience.

---

# User Interface Review

Review the visual quality of the implementation.

Verify:

* Visual hierarchy
* Layout consistency
* Spacing
* Typography
* Component consistency
* Color usage
* Responsive behavior
* Loading states
* Empty states
* Error states
* Permission states

Every interface should feel like part of the same product.

---

# User Experience Review

Review the usability of the implementation.

Verify:

* Primary actions are immediately obvious.
* User workflows remain efficient.
* Navigation is intuitive.
* Cognitive load is minimized.
* Important information is easy to discover.
* User feedback is clear and timely.

Engineering decisions should improve user experience rather than increase implementation complexity.

---

# Accessibility Review

Accessibility is a fundamental aspect of engineering quality and should never be treated as an optional enhancement.

Review should verify:

* Keyboard accessibility
* Visible focus indicators
* Semantic HTML
* ARIA usage where appropriate
* Color contrast
* Touch target sizing
* Screen reader compatibility
* Accessible form validation

Every feature should remain usable regardless of a user's input method or accessibility requirements.

---

# Responsive Review

Every supported screen size should provide a complete and usable experience.

Review should verify:

* Desktop usability
* Tablet usability
* Mobile usability
* Responsive layouts
* Adaptive navigation
* Readable typography
* Appropriate spacing
* Viewport-safe dialogs
* Scroll behavior
* Usable data tables

Responsive behavior should preserve usability rather than simply resize the interface.

---

# Performance Review

Review should identify implementation choices that negatively affect application performance.

Verify:

* Rendering efficiency
* Client component usage
* Server component opportunities
* Bundle size
* Network requests
* Data fetching strategy
* Expensive computations
* Lazy loading opportunities
* Resource optimization

Performance optimization should focus on measurable user experience improvements rather than premature optimization.

---

# Security Review

Every implementation should be reviewed with a security-first mindset.

Verify:

* Authentication
* Authorization
* Input validation
* Output encoding
* Sensitive data handling
* Server-side authorization
* API protection
* Database access control
* Secret management
* Principle of least privilege

Client-side validation should never be considered sufficient protection.

---

# Code Quality Review

Review should evaluate long-term maintainability rather than immediate functionality.

Verify:

* Readability
* Naming consistency
* Separation of concerns
* Appropriate abstraction
* Code duplication
* Component responsibility
* Reusability
* Simplicity
* Testability
* Maintainability

Every implementation should be understandable without requiring additional explanation.

Readable code should always be preferred over clever code.

---

# Review Quality Gates

An implementation is considered review-complete only when all applicable review categories satisfy the expected engineering standards.

Every review should verify that:

* Business objectives are satisfied.
* Product principles remain consistent.
* Architectural boundaries are respected.
* User experience remains intuitive.
* Accessibility requirements are considered.
* Responsive behavior is verified.
* Performance remains acceptable.
* Security risks have been addressed.
* Code quality meets long-term maintainability expectations.
* Relevant Engineering Standards have been followed.

If any applicable review category fails, the implementation should return to refinement before approval.

---

# Production Readiness

Production readiness represents the minimum quality required before an implementation becomes part of the production system.

Every production-ready implementation should include, when applicable:

* Loading state
* Empty state
* Error state
* Success feedback
* Permission handling
* Responsive layout
* Accessibility support
* Type safety
* Validation
* Logging
* Error handling
* Reusable implementation
* Maintainable architecture
* Documentation updates

Production readiness is achieved only when both technical quality and user experience meet the expectations defined by the Engineering Standards.

---

# Review Completion

A review is complete only when all applicable Engineering Standards have been satisfied.

Review completion requires confirmation that:

* The implementation fulfills the requested requirements.
* No constitutional principles have been violated.
* Repository conventions are respected.
* Product direction remains consistent.
* Architectural integrity is preserved.
* No critical engineering concerns remain unresolved.

Review completion indicates engineering confidence, not merely task completion.

---

# AI Review Responsibilities

Before completing any implementation, AI should perform a complete internal review.

The review should:

* Evaluate the implementation against all applicable Engineering Standards.
* Detect opportunities for simplification.
* Remove unnecessary complexity.
* Improve consistency.
* Resolve quality concerns before presenting the result.

AI should continuously optimize for implementation quality rather than generation speed.

Review is an integral part of implementation, not a separate activity performed afterward.

---

# Closing Principle

Engineering quality is measured by the maintainability, reliability, and consistency of the final implementation rather than the speed at which it was produced.

Every implementation should be reviewed as if it will become a permanent part of the product.

Deliver only work that is ready to be maintained, extended, and deployed with confidence.
